In C, you can terminate a program using the `exit()` function from the `<stdlib.h>` header. Here's how you can use it:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main() {
    // Your code here

    // Terminate the program
    exit(0);

    // This line will not be executed
    printf("This line will not be printed\n");

    return 0;
}
```

In the above code, `exit(0)` is used to terminate the program. The `exit(0)` function is used to terminate the program with a status of 0, which indicates successful termination. You can also use `exit(1)` to terminate the program with a status of 1, which indicates an error.
